## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of tri-valued constraint systems with a custom operator and backtracking propagation, ensuring candidates reason about Kleene semantics and propagation rules.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic with T > U > F; ∧/∨ act as min/max.
- Custom operator Nand defined as not(and(x,y)).
- Backtracking propagation to prune domains and find a satisfying assignment.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Basic tri-valued lattice: T > U > F
const rank = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return rank[a] <= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return rank[a] >= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function nand(a,b){ return not(and(a,b)); }
function implies(p,q){ return or(not(p), q); }

// sample assignment
let F1='T', F2='F', F3='U', F4='F';
console.log(implies(or(F1,F2), F3)); // U
console.log(and(F2,F4)); // F
console.log(nand(F3,F4)); // T
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to N flags and arbitrary constraint sets?
- What changes when moving to a 4-valued logic or alternative lattices?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 3-valued dataflow reasoning, fixpoint propagation, and translating logic into a workable algorithm.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic (T, F, U)
- Worklist/fixpoint propagation
- Dataflow dependency graphs
- Handling cycles and convergence

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued logic
const T = true, F = false, U = 'U';
function not(x){ if (x===T) return F; if (x===F) return T; return U; }
function and(a,b){ if (a===F || b===F) return F; if (a===T && b===T) return T; return U; }
function or(a,b){ if (a===T || b===T) return T; if (a===F && b===F) return F; return U; }

// program: A = B AND C; D = NOT E; F = D OR A
let B = U, C = T, E = U;
let A = and(B, C);
let D = not(E);
let F = or(D, A);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to cycles and determine worst-case iterations?
- How would you optimize for sparse graphs and pruning impossible combinations?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued logic, especially how disjunction, conjunction, and implication interact under uncertainty. It also probes backtracking strategies in a non-classical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued semantics and default propagation
- Backtracking with partial assignments
- Constraint propagation under non-monotonic connectives

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued helpers (short version)
const vals = ['T','F','U'];
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function or(a,b){ return (a==='T' || b==='T')?'T': (a==='F' && b==='F')?'F':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return (a==='F' || b==='F')?'F': (a==='T' && b==='T')?'T':'U'; }
function imp(a,b){ // a→b := ¬a ∨ b
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued semantics in a concrete constraint system, plus practical propagation and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬U=U; a→b = (¬a) ∨ b; ∨/∧ as max/min.
- Constraint propagation: prune inconsistent values; arc-consistency.
- Backtracking: search space pruning when a constraint becomes F.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued semantics helpers (T=1, U=0.5, F=0)
const V = { T: 1, U: 0.5, F: 0 };
const notV = (v) => (v === V.T ? V.F : v === V.F ? V.T : V.U);
const orV = (a,b) => Math.max(a,b);
const andV = (a,b) => Math.min(a,b);
const implies = (a,b) => orV(notV(a), b);
```

